      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A memory leak in an automotive control unit, how frustrating. You're right, it's not like a mischievous goblin sneaking into your code and eating all the memory, but it's a similar feeling of being outsmarted by a sneaky little bug. To track down the leak, I'd recommend using a combination of tools and techniques. \n\nFirst, enable the heap debugger in your IDE, it's like having a trusty sidekick, like a goblin scout, that helps you navigate the complex world of memory management. This will give you detailed information about the memory allocations and deallocations in your program.\n\nNext, use a memory profiling tool, such as Valgrind or AddressSanitizer, to identify the memory leaks. These tools are like goblin traps, they'll catch the memory leaks and give you a clear picture of where they're happening. You can run your program under these tools to see which functions are allocating memory that's not being freed.\n\nAnother approach is to use a memory leak detection library, such as MemoryLeakDetector or LeakSanitizer, which can help you identify memory leaks in your code. These libraries are like goblin detectors, they'll sniff out the memory leaks and give you a",
